-[ Talos backport note (not part of the upstream commit message):
-  Cherry-picked from mainline commit 0cc425f18f59 ("net: cadence: macb:
-  implement EEE TX LPI support"), merged in Linux 7.1, onto 6.18.41.
-
-  Two context conflicts were resolved; neither changes the behaviour of
-  the upstream patch:
-
-  1. macb.h capability mask.  Upstream defines MACB_CAPS_EEE as BIT(25).
-     6.18.41 still uses the older hex form of this list and has none of
-     the BIT(22)..BIT(24) capabilities added after 6.18, and BIT(25) is
-     already taken there by MACB_CAPS_HIGH_SPEED (0x02000000).  Reusing
-     the upstream value would have made RP1 test true for
-     MACB_CAPS_HIGH_SPEED and register PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_10GBASER /
-     MAC_10000FD.  MACB_CAPS_EEE is therefore defined here as
-     0x00004000, the lowest free bit on 6.18.41, matching the
-     surrounding hex style.  The value of a capability bit is private to
-     the driver, so this has no effect beyond the driver.
-
-  2. macb_start_xmit() LPI wake.  0001-net-macb-flush-PCIe-posted-write-
-     after-TSTART-doorbe.patch already inserts a PCIe posted-write
-     read-back immediately after the TSTART write in this same
-     spin_lock(&bp->lock) region.  macb_tx_lpi_wake() is placed before
-     the TSTART write, as upstream does, so the resulting order is
-     LPI wake -> TSTART -> posted-write flush.  Both changes are
-     preserved. ]
